So notice, this would be like the forecast for 93

50
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:12,480
being regressed on the actual observed inflation in 1993.

51
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:15,680
The forecast, of course, come before 1993,

52
00:22:15,680 --> 00:22:17,080
by whatever happened in 1973.

53
00:22:17,080 --> 00:22:19,360
But in the regression, we go and take

54
00:22:19,360 --> 00:22:22,240
the forecast for the year in question.

55
00:22:22,240 --> 00:22:25,120
And then compare them to what actually happened.

56
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:27,880
And rational explanations implies two things,

57
00:22:27,880 --> 00:22:31,120
alpha equals 0 and beta equals 1.

58
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:32,960
If you've ever done an hypothesis test where

59
00:22:32,960 --> 00:22:35,600
you have two conditions that you're testing,

60
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:37,600
that's what they were doing.

61
00:22:37,600 --> 00:22:41,880
An F test that you have to do if you've forgotten F tests.

62
00:22:41,880 --> 00:22:44,400
And basically, what this means is that, first of all,

63
00:22:44,400 --> 00:22:48,640
on average, the forecast equals the truth.

64
00:22:48,640 --> 00:22:50,680
And then furthermore, if the truth goes up

65
00:22:50,680 --> 00:22:52,560
by 1 percentage point, the forecast goes up

66
00:22:52,560 --> 00:22:53,960
by 1 percentage point.

67
00:22:53,960 --> 00:22:55,800
So if there were a positive value of alpha,

68
00:22:55,800 --> 00:22:58,520
this would mean that would mean there's a general tendency

69
00:22:58,520 --> 00:23:00,040
to overestimate.

70
00:23:00,040 --> 00:23:02,560
Negative alpha, general tendency to underestimate.

71
00:23:02,560 --> 00:23:04,720
And then again, if beta were greater than 1,

72
00:23:04,720 --> 00:23:06,560
this would mean that when inflation goes up

73
00:23:06,560 --> 00:23:08,240
by a point, by 1 percentage point,

74
00:23:08,240 --> 00:23:11,040
forecasts are going up by more than that.

75
00:23:11,040 --> 00:23:15,040
So that's what the rational expectations forecast would be.

76
00:23:15,040 --> 00:23:19,600
And you could imagine doing any number of macroeconomic

77
00:23:19,600 --> 00:23:23,560
or other forecasts and testing for rational expectations.

78
00:23:30,280 --> 00:23:32,640
Now, something that's been very interesting to me

79
00:23:32,640 --> 00:23:35,520
is testing economic beliefs themselves

80
00:23:35,520 --> 00:23:37,440
for rational expectations.

81
00:23:37,440 --> 00:23:39,880
Because at least when I got into economics,

82
00:23:39,880 --> 00:23:42,200
the reason why I liked it is because the economists

83
00:23:42,200 --> 00:23:44,880
were telling me the opposite of what other everyone else had

84
00:23:44,880 --> 00:23:46,000
told me.

85
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:47,360
That's why I liked it.

86
00:23:47,360 --> 00:23:48,840
So I liked economists saying, you

87
00:23:48,840 --> 00:23:51,600
know all the stuff that teachers and parents and media tell you?

88
00:23:51,600 --> 00:23:53,080
Wrong.

89
00:23:53,080 --> 00:23:55,880
And not just sometimes too high, sometimes too low,

90
00:23:55,880 --> 00:23:57,360
but predictably wrong.

91
00:23:57,360 --> 00:23:59,480
Like the economist saying, you know

92
00:23:59,480 --> 00:24:01,800
all this complaining about free trade you've heard?

93
00:24:01,800 --> 00:24:02,300
Wrong.

94
00:24:02,300 --> 00:24:04,560
Free trade's great.

95
00:24:04,560 --> 00:24:08,920
Or you know all this stuff about how the more regulation

96
00:24:08,920 --> 00:24:10,040
of drugs, the better?

97
00:24:10,040 --> 00:24:10,680
Wrong.

98
00:24:10,680 --> 00:24:12,480
Regulation of drugs can kill people.

99
00:24:12,480 --> 00:24:14,280
Here's how.

100
00:24:14,280 --> 00:24:17,720
You're murdering people with regulation.

101
00:24:17,720 --> 00:24:19,640
Negligent manslaughter.

102
00:24:19,640 --> 00:24:21,560
Anyway.

103
00:24:21,560 --> 00:24:24,560
Or you know how everyone's always

104
00:24:24,560 --> 00:24:27,600
talking about the disemployment effect of the minimum wage?

105
00:24:27,600 --> 00:24:28,200
No.

106
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:30,200
Exactly.

107
00:24:30,200 --> 00:24:31,840
They should be.

108
00:24:31,840 --> 00:24:34,360
They're acting like there's no disemployment effect.

109
00:24:34,360 --> 00:24:36,800
But there's got to be a disemployment effect.

110
00:24:36,800 --> 00:24:40,960
A minimum wage is $1 million an hour.

111
00:24:40,960 --> 00:24:43,560
So anyway, that's what got me interested in economics

112
00:24:43,560 --> 00:24:46,680
was economists going and saying, these

113
00:24:46,680 --> 00:24:51,440
are the results of our study or our work, which

114
00:24:51,440 --> 00:24:54,200
can paint a very different picture than the regular one

115
00:24:54,200 --> 00:24:57,480
in a very predictable direction.

116
00:24:57,480 --> 00:24:59,680
So anyway, this is why I got excited.

117
00:24:59,680 --> 00:25:02,000
Now the fact that it excited me doesn't mean it's true.

118
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:03,280
Unfortunately.

119
00:25:03,280 --> 00:25:05,680
The world should work that way, but it does not

120
00:25:05,680 --> 00:25:07,680
have to work that way.

121
00:25:07,680 --> 00:25:09,280
And when I got into grad school and I

122
00:25:09,280 --> 00:25:11,880
tried making any of the arguments that got me

123
00:25:11,880 --> 00:25:14,480
interested in economics, normally people would just say,

124
00:25:14,480 --> 00:25:16,680
now this.

125
00:25:16,680 --> 00:25:18,720
So you can't say that people underestimate

126
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:20,400
the benefits of free trade.

127
00:25:20,400 --> 00:25:23,240
No, you must assume that on average they're correct.

128
00:25:23,240 --> 00:25:25,360
All you can say is maybe sometimes they

129
00:25:25,360 --> 00:25:27,400
might be too high, sometimes they may be too low.

130
00:25:27,400 --> 00:25:30,520
That's all you're allowed to say in a rational explanations

131
00:25:30,520 --> 00:25:31,020
framework.

132
00:25:31,020 --> 00:25:32,800
But maybe rational explanations is wrong.

133
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:37,760
All kinds.

134
00:25:37,760 --> 00:25:42,800
Anyway, so when I started working on this,

135
00:25:42,800 --> 00:25:45,320
I expected there to be at least some decent empirics.

136
00:25:45,320 --> 00:25:47,720
Really, a lot of cases there's none.

137
00:25:47,720 --> 00:25:50,760
And this is more of just a convenient assumption.

138
00:25:50,760 --> 00:25:53,040
And it can be convenient in two ways.

139
00:25:53,040 --> 00:25:54,920
One is it's just empirically convenient

140
00:25:54,920 --> 00:25:56,920
because if this is the way the whole world works,

141
00:25:56,920 --> 00:25:59,440
there's so many questions you don't have to study anymore.

142
00:25:59,440 --> 00:26:01,280
So I went into it like, well, let's just

143
00:26:01,280 --> 00:26:03,400
find what the truth is, and then we know on average

144
00:26:03,400 --> 00:26:05,080
that's what everybody believes.

145
00:26:05,080 --> 00:26:08,160
So that simplifies your life a lot.

146
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:09,720
And then of course, if you just like

147
00:26:09,720 --> 00:26:12,320
to think the world works fine the way that it is,

148
00:26:12,320 --> 00:26:14,280
this is also very politically convenient.

149
00:26:14,280 --> 00:26:17,160
So you just want to put your blessing on the status quo,

150
00:26:17,160 --> 00:26:19,480
then very naturally something like this

151
00:26:19,480 --> 00:26:20,560
is going to appeal to you.

152
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:23,720
But I say, look, if most people think that education is good,

153
00:26:23,720 --> 00:26:26,000
that means it's good.

154
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:29,240
And if you disagree, then almost certainly you're wrong.

155
00:26:29,240 --> 00:26:31,160
It's like, what are the odds that you're right

156
00:26:31,160 --> 00:26:32,560
and everyone else is wrong?

157
00:26:32,560 --> 00:26:34,080
It's like, oh, man.

158
00:26:34,080 --> 00:26:36,360
If that's where I ran, I would never have gone into economics

159
00:26:36,360 --> 00:26:37,040
if that's what I thought.

160
00:26:37,040 --> 00:26:38,680
I would have found some other area where most people were

161
00:26:38,680 --> 00:26:39,680
screwed out then.

162
00:26:39,680 --> 00:26:41,240
Would have moved into that.

163
00:26:41,240 --> 00:26:44,840
But anyway, so this is what I was dealing with.

164
00:26:44,840 --> 00:26:47,200
So one of the first projects in the undergraduate school

165
00:26:47,200 --> 00:26:49,800
was actually trying to get some data on this.

166
00:26:49,800 --> 00:26:51,440
And it turned out some other people

167
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:53,840
had already created all the data, which

168
00:26:53,840 --> 00:26:56,400
was a very expensive data set, hundreds of thousands of

169
00:26:56,400 --> 00:26:57,320
dollars.

170
00:26:57,320 --> 00:26:58,760
And then they published one paper

171
00:26:58,760 --> 00:27:01,240
in the Journal of Economic Perspectives using the data,

172
00:27:01,240 --> 00:27:03,160
and then dropped the whole topic.

173
00:27:03,160 --> 00:27:06,200
And when I asked, they just gave me the data for free.

174
00:27:06,200 --> 00:27:09,600
And out of that, I got six papers, a book.

175
00:27:09,600 --> 00:27:13,200
So this is the kind of thing, don't look a gift horse

176
00:27:13,200 --> 00:27:13,760
in the mouth.

177
00:27:13,760 --> 00:27:16,000
Don't say, well, why did you do this whole study

178
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,200
if you were barely willing to write one paper on it?

179
00:27:18,200 --> 00:27:20,720
Instead, just say, thank you for sharing.

180
00:27:20,720 --> 00:27:23,680
And a more general lesson, there are actually

181
00:27:23,680 --> 00:27:26,400
a lot of data sets that are out there that

182
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:28,640
have barely been used.

183
00:27:28,640 --> 00:27:30,920
So for example, there are at least a lot of variables

184
00:27:30,920 --> 00:27:32,160
that have barely been used.

185
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:34,080
I think it's confusing as to what's going on.

186
00:27:34,080 --> 00:27:37,080
Presumably, people just had more money than they had questions.

187
00:27:37,080 --> 00:27:39,640
So they needed to know about the questions they cared about.

188
00:27:39,640 --> 00:27:41,560
So for example, in the General Social Survey,

189
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:44,280
if you go look at that, if you go to Google Scholar

190
00:27:44,280 --> 00:27:47,120
and just put in different variable names,

191
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:49,200
it's got to be a variable name that's distinctive,

192
00:27:49,200 --> 00:27:52,000
like the word help poor, one word.

193
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:52,960
So it's not a lot.

194
00:27:52,960 --> 00:27:55,040
Other than the General Social Survey,

195
00:27:55,040 --> 00:27:57,480
there's not a lot of people who use that word.

196
00:27:57,480 --> 00:27:59,440
In fact, it's probably only there.

197
00:27:59,440 --> 00:28:01,440
If you go to Google Scholar and put that in,

198
00:28:01,440 --> 00:28:03,000
you'll find there are many questions

199
00:28:03,000 --> 00:28:05,000
in the General Social Survey that no one has ever

200
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:08,040
written a paper about, ever.

201
00:28:08,040 --> 00:28:12,760
So why shouldn't you be the person that writes that paper?

202
00:28:12,760 --> 00:28:15,600
And no one has ever done this.

203
00:28:15,600 --> 00:28:17,120
And that's original.

204
00:28:17,120 --> 00:28:21,640
If no one's ever done it, that's called original research.

205
00:28:21,640 --> 00:28:25,720
And what rewards do we give out for original research?

206
00:28:25,720 --> 00:28:26,220
Citations.

207
00:28:26,220 --> 00:28:27,560
It's a job for life.

208
00:28:27,560 --> 00:28:28,560
So yes.

209
00:28:28,560 --> 00:28:35,520
So it's all, of course, when you find these 20 door

210
00:28:35,520 --> 00:28:36,920
bills sitting on the sidewalk, you

211
00:28:36,920 --> 00:28:38,520
don't want to make sure are they real.

212
00:28:38,520 --> 00:28:41,280
But if they are, well, great.

213
00:28:41,280 --> 00:28:42,440
Don't say, how can this be?

214
00:28:42,440 --> 00:28:43,440
Go for it.

215
00:28:43,440 --> 00:28:44,840
Anyway.

216
00:28:44,840 --> 00:28:47,240
So there was something called the Survey of Americans

217
00:28:47,240 --> 00:28:49,560
that Consumed the Economy that asked economists

218
00:28:49,560 --> 00:28:52,280
and the general public identical questions about how

219
00:28:52,280 --> 00:28:53,760
the economy works.

220
00:28:53,760 --> 00:28:55,600
And I got this data set.

221
00:28:55,600 --> 00:28:58,600
And then, right setting up tests for rationalizations

222
00:28:58,600 --> 00:29:00,400
for the public's economic beliefs

223
00:29:00,400 --> 00:29:05,240
to go and do some empirical tests on the kinds of stories

224
00:29:05,240 --> 00:29:08,000
that got me interested in economics in the first place.

225
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:09,920
And a very natural way to do it is

226
00:29:09,920 --> 00:29:13,880
you got some measure of the beliefs of the public.

227
00:29:13,880 --> 00:29:15,440
And now, this is the endemic.

228
00:29:15,440 --> 00:29:19,360
So here we actually have forecast and truth.

229
00:29:19,360 --> 00:29:21,920
Now, unfortunately, for how the economy works,

230
00:29:21,920 --> 00:29:26,040
it's hard to get an uncontroversial measure of truth.

231
00:29:26,040 --> 00:29:27,680
So how can you do empirics?

232
00:29:27,680 --> 00:29:29,400
Well, we can do this.

233
00:29:29,400 --> 00:29:30,880
We can at least say, look, according

234
00:29:30,880 --> 00:29:33,600
to rational expectations, laymen and experts

235
00:29:33,600 --> 00:29:35,160
should have the same average belief.

236
00:29:37,720 --> 00:29:40,320
Because all that should be occurring

237
00:29:40,320 --> 00:29:42,480
when people learn more is that it should be compressing

238
00:29:42,480 --> 00:29:45,840
beliefs around the truth without changing the meaning.

239
00:29:45,840 --> 00:29:48,800
So we can go and test rational explanations

240
00:29:48,800 --> 00:29:51,160
by going and getting people's beliefs

241
00:29:51,160 --> 00:29:55,160
and regressing on a constant plus a coefficient times

242
00:29:55,160 --> 00:29:58,160
a dummy variable for whether you're an economist, where

243
00:29:58,160 --> 00:30:05,320
1 equals you are and 0 equals I don't know.

244
00:30:05,320 --> 00:30:07,680
Now, these regressions leave open the possibility

245
00:30:07,680 --> 00:30:10,120
that it's economists who are biased and the general public

246
00:30:10,120 --> 00:30:11,440
that is correct.

247
00:30:11,440 --> 00:30:13,080
So there is that problem.

248
00:30:13,080 --> 00:30:16,280
At least we can say one group is wrong.

249
00:30:16,280 --> 00:30:23,000
And then we can also entertain arguments either way.

250
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,400
So there is the argument that when people study a subject

251
00:30:25,400 --> 00:30:27,560
for a long time, they generally know more about it.

252
00:30:27,560 --> 00:30:29,480
It seems like a good starting place.

253
00:30:29,480 --> 00:30:31,640
There are counter-arguments about why experts might be

254
00:30:31,640 --> 00:30:33,040
biased, but that's false.

255
00:30:33,040 --> 00:30:34,880
Yeah, you had a question?

256
00:30:34,880 --> 00:30:36,600
What if both groups are wrong?

257
00:30:36,600 --> 00:30:37,600
Yeah.

258
00:30:37,600 --> 00:30:39,520
Well, so that's another possibility.

259
00:30:39,520 --> 00:30:40,960
So we could just find them both.

260
00:30:40,960 --> 00:30:43,360
So actually, even if they had the same view, it's possible.

261
00:30:43,360 --> 00:30:44,960
It's possible that they're both wrong.

262
00:30:44,960 --> 00:30:48,080
But this is still a test of rationalizations

263
00:30:48,080 --> 00:30:52,000
in that it is possible we'll find one or the other is wrong.

264
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,440
But yeah, it could be that they're equal,

265
00:30:54,440 --> 00:30:56,600
and then rationalization still fails.

266
00:30:56,600 --> 00:30:58,240
But yeah, as soon as it's a case where

267
00:30:58,240 --> 00:31:01,400
we can say the rationalization is wrong for a group,

268
00:31:01,400 --> 00:31:05,440
but still you can't get the decisive proof that it's right.

269
00:31:05,440 --> 00:31:09,920
But can we ask some questions, like not the 1, like 0, 1, 2,

270
00:31:09,920 --> 00:31:12,680
but some actual questions like the inflation?

271
00:31:12,680 --> 00:31:13,800
Yeah, we can say the same.

272
00:31:13,800 --> 00:31:15,600
You ran the regression.

273
00:31:15,600 --> 00:31:17,480
So you can do that, too.

274
00:31:17,480 --> 00:31:20,680
There were very few questions like that in this data set.

275
00:31:20,680 --> 00:31:22,560
So there are other data sets out there,

276
00:31:22,560 --> 00:31:25,720
which I was going to say give very similar results.

277
00:31:25,720 --> 00:31:27,560
What was nice about this is that they

278
00:31:27,560 --> 00:31:30,360
asked exactly the same questions, both groups.

279
00:31:30,360 --> 00:31:32,240
So you can't just say that they're

280
00:31:32,240 --> 00:31:33,760
answering different questions.

281
00:31:33,760 --> 00:31:35,600
Because there's a lot of surveys of the economic beliefs

282
00:31:35,600 --> 00:31:36,840
of the general public.

283
00:31:36,840 --> 00:31:38,600
And then there's totally different surveys

284
00:31:38,600 --> 00:31:40,280
of the economic beliefs of economists.

285
00:31:40,280 --> 00:31:42,680
And you might say the questions are similar.

286
00:31:42,680 --> 00:31:44,560
But again, someone could say, well, maybe they

287
00:31:44,560 --> 00:31:46,120
just gave different answers because the questions

288
00:31:46,120 --> 00:31:47,000
were different.

289
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:49,920
I mean, it reflects the belief on the actual data.

290
00:31:49,920 --> 00:31:50,840
Pardon?

291
00:31:50,840 --> 00:31:54,640
Yeah, yeah, yeah, so that is another thing

292
00:31:54,640 --> 00:31:55,440
that can be done.

293
00:31:55,440 --> 00:31:57,680
Actually, in my book, I do talk about cases like that.

294
00:31:57,680 --> 00:31:59,880
Ones where you have like the budget, budget numbers,

295
00:31:59,880 --> 00:32:01,040
for example.

296
00:32:01,040 --> 00:32:03,080
Writing it there, you will see that the public

297
00:32:03,080 --> 00:32:06,920
has really crazy views about the budget and very predictable.

298
00:32:06,920 --> 00:32:08,320
Essentially, the kinds of spending

299
00:32:08,320 --> 00:32:09,760
that they think are out of control

300
00:32:09,760 --> 00:32:12,960
are the kinds of spending they don't like.

301
00:32:12,960 --> 00:32:15,320
So there's a general view that foreign aid spending,

302
00:32:15,320 --> 00:32:18,960
which is a popular, is also enormous.

303
00:32:18,960 --> 00:32:23,320
And if they didn't give numbers, then you might just say, well,

304
00:32:23,320 --> 00:32:24,560
they're speaking politically.

305
00:32:24,560 --> 00:32:27,160
Because you think it's bad, then any amount excessive

306
00:32:27,160 --> 00:32:27,680
is too much.

307
00:32:27,680 --> 00:32:30,840
But as a percentage of the budget, how much is it?

308
00:32:30,840 --> 00:32:34,800
The numbers the public gives are absurdly large.

309
00:32:34,800 --> 00:32:36,680
You're wondering, how do they even do this?

310
00:32:36,680 --> 00:32:38,760
Seems to me this is what you call moral causation,

311
00:32:38,760 --> 00:32:40,240
like who is bad?

312
00:32:40,240 --> 00:32:42,200
And then from this, I will deduce

313
00:32:42,200 --> 00:32:45,840
what their quantitative importance is.

314
00:32:45,840 --> 00:32:47,360
You can't do that.

315
00:32:47,360 --> 00:32:48,240
