The other thing happening, you get $0 of market income,

51
01:22:20,000 --> 01:22:22,600
and you still pay the policy.

52
01:22:22,600 --> 01:22:25,480
So you also have to pay the premium even in bad years.

53
01:22:25,480 --> 01:22:26,840
Good years, bad years, you always

54
01:22:26,840 --> 01:22:28,880
write the check for the premium.

55
01:22:28,880 --> 01:22:31,720
But the good news is in the bad state of affairs,

56
01:22:31,720 --> 01:22:34,840
the insurance company gives you i dollars.

57
01:22:34,840 --> 01:22:40,880
So you bought i dollars at a cost of 0.4 times x times i,

58
01:22:40,880 --> 01:22:48,920
where x is whatever the market for overhead.

59
01:22:48,920 --> 01:22:53,920
And then we need to raise that to the 0.8 power.

60
01:22:53,920 --> 01:22:59,120
So this is a bit messy, but nothing

61
01:22:59,120 --> 01:23:00,960
that you haven't encountered before.

62
01:23:00,960 --> 01:23:04,760
So if we just simplify it, and then we differentiate it,

63
01:23:04,760 --> 01:23:06,840
and then we set it all equal to 0,

64
01:23:06,840 --> 01:23:10,640
then we will get the mess at the top of the next page.

65
01:23:10,640 --> 01:23:15,840
So you get 0.6 times 0.8 times negative 0.4x times

66
01:23:15,840 --> 01:23:19,320
that thing inside raised to the negative 0.2 power.

67
01:23:19,320 --> 01:23:22,920
Everyone remember the following negative 0.2 powers.

68
01:23:22,920 --> 01:23:28,800
And then plus 0.4 times 0.8 times 1 minus 0.4x times

69
01:23:28,800 --> 01:23:31,040
all that mess also raised to that.

70
01:23:31,040 --> 01:23:33,160
You also raised to the negative 0.2 power.

71
01:23:33,160 --> 01:23:35,320
That equals 0.

72
01:23:35,320 --> 01:23:37,000
Now, if you're getting very nervous,

73
01:23:37,000 --> 01:23:39,360
the good news is if you've got something

74
01:23:39,360 --> 01:23:41,800
raised to a weird power plus another thing raised

75
01:23:41,800 --> 01:23:44,400
to a weird power all equal to 0, you can generally

76
01:23:44,400 --> 01:23:47,480
go and separate it out and then rearrange terms.

77
01:23:47,480 --> 01:23:49,520
And then you just get one expression

78
01:23:49,520 --> 01:23:53,160
raised to that weird negative 0.2 power.

79
01:23:53,160 --> 01:23:57,160
So if you do, you would see that in line E.

80
01:23:57,160 --> 01:23:59,880
And then all you have to do is take the negative 0.2

81
01:23:59,880 --> 01:24:01,760
root of both sides.

82
01:24:01,760 --> 01:24:04,280
Remember when's the last time you took the negative 0.2

83
01:24:04,280 --> 01:24:05,720
root of something?

84
01:24:05,720 --> 01:24:07,400
Like I said, it's probably been a while.

85
01:24:07,400 --> 01:24:08,240
But it can be done.

86
01:24:08,240 --> 01:24:08,860
You're familiar.

87
01:24:08,860 --> 01:24:11,000
You remember how these things are all possible.

88
01:24:11,000 --> 01:24:13,880
You can take any kind of odd root that you want.

89
01:24:13,880 --> 01:24:16,000
It's all doable.

90
01:24:16,000 --> 01:24:19,200
And then if we go and define theta

91
01:24:19,200 --> 01:24:23,280
as being that mess there, all raised to the negative 0.5

92
01:24:23,280 --> 01:24:26,840
power, we get the answer.

93
01:24:31,120 --> 01:24:35,520
So we get I star, which is the optimal amount of dollars

94
01:24:35,520 --> 01:24:41,480
insurance to buy, will be equal to 1,000 all over theta times

95
01:24:41,480 --> 01:24:46,760
1 minus 0.4x plus 0.4x.

96
01:24:46,760 --> 01:24:48,120
All right.

97
01:24:48,120 --> 01:24:51,000
Now, at this point, this probably still

98
01:24:51,000 --> 01:24:53,040
seems like it isn't really telling you anything

99
01:24:53,040 --> 01:24:53,540
interesting.

100
01:24:53,540 --> 01:24:55,680
And especially this hour a day, you

101
01:24:55,680 --> 01:24:58,040
may be baffled by the math or what in the world

102
01:24:58,040 --> 01:24:59,360
is going on here.

103
01:24:59,360 --> 01:25:01,520
But here is something interesting,

104
01:25:01,520 --> 01:25:06,240
which is that if the insurance really is actually fair,

105
01:25:06,240 --> 01:25:08,560
so x equals 1, then that theta thing

106
01:25:08,560 --> 01:25:11,280
simplifies down very nicely.

107
01:25:11,280 --> 01:25:12,760
So in fact, theta then becomes.

108
01:25:12,760 --> 01:25:16,480
So if x is 1, then theta is equal to 1.

109
01:25:16,480 --> 01:25:18,840
And if theta is 1, then look at how nicely everything

110
01:25:18,840 --> 01:25:20,160
simplifies.

111
01:25:20,160 --> 01:25:21,680
Because then we've got 1.

112
01:25:21,680 --> 01:25:22,480
So we have 1.

113
01:25:22,480 --> 01:25:23,240
So that's 1.

114
01:25:23,240 --> 01:25:27,000
Then this can be 1 minus 0.4x plus 0.4x.

115
01:25:27,000 --> 01:25:28,640
The 0.4x is canceled.

116
01:25:28,640 --> 01:25:31,600
That leaves 1 in the denominator.

117
01:25:31,600 --> 01:25:35,040
And then I star is going to just be equal to 1,000.

118
01:25:35,040 --> 01:25:38,520
So this says that if there's actuarially fair insurance,

119
01:25:38,520 --> 01:25:41,880
then a risk averse person will fully insure.

120
01:25:41,880 --> 01:25:47,040
They will buy $1,000 worth of insurance at a cost of $400.

121
01:25:47,040 --> 01:25:51,280
And then they will have $600 regardless

122
01:25:51,280 --> 01:25:53,360
of the state of the world.

123
01:25:53,360 --> 01:25:55,780
And that's exactly what you'd expect a risk averse person

124
01:25:55,780 --> 01:25:58,600
to do if there's actually a fair insurance around.

125
01:25:58,600 --> 01:26:00,960
Because basically, what is actually a fair insurance?

126
01:26:00,960 --> 01:26:03,520
It is an opportunity to get the average income

127
01:26:03,520 --> 01:26:06,360
at every possible state of the world.

128
01:26:06,360 --> 01:26:09,440
And if you're risk averse, isn't that exactly what you would want?

129
01:26:09,440 --> 01:26:09,940
Of course.

130
01:26:09,940 --> 01:26:11,480
So that makes sense.

131
01:26:11,480 --> 01:26:14,040
And then as you increase the markup, as you might guess,

132
01:26:14,040 --> 01:26:16,200
this means that you'll buy less than that insurance.

133
01:26:16,200 --> 01:26:17,960
So even though you're risk averse,

134
01:26:17,960 --> 01:26:20,400
as the price of insurance goes up,

135
01:26:20,400 --> 01:26:24,680
you are willing to tolerate more and more risk.

136
01:26:24,680 --> 01:26:27,480
Make sense?

137
01:26:27,480 --> 01:26:30,640
And then if x were to get really high,

138
01:26:30,640 --> 01:26:34,920
even risk averse people wouldn't want to buy any insurance.

139
01:26:34,920 --> 01:26:36,180
Wouldn't want to buy insurance at all.

140
01:26:36,180 --> 01:26:40,480
And instead, they would go and sell insurance to others.

141
01:26:40,480 --> 01:26:44,560
Who would risk averse people sell insurance to?

142
01:26:44,560 --> 01:26:46,200
People who are?

143
01:26:46,200 --> 01:26:47,480
More risk averse?

144
01:26:47,480 --> 01:26:50,800
Well, even more risk averse.

145
01:26:50,800 --> 01:26:54,720
Yes, so the people that have w raised to the 0.8 utility

146
01:26:54,720 --> 01:26:57,280
function might sell insurance to the people with a w raised

147
01:26:57,280 --> 01:26:59,440
to the 0.7 utility function.

148
01:26:59,440 --> 01:27:01,920
Or w raised to the 0.1.

149
01:27:01,920 --> 01:27:04,840
So it is possible, then, you could have insurance markets

150
01:27:04,840 --> 01:27:07,080
in a world where everyone's risk averse.

151
01:27:07,080 --> 01:27:08,600
Just like in a world where everyone

152
01:27:08,600 --> 01:27:10,080
hates the smell of garbage, there's

153
01:27:10,080 --> 01:27:12,000
still going to be some garbage men.

154
01:27:12,000 --> 01:27:13,500
And who will the garbage men generally

155
01:27:13,500 --> 01:27:15,920
be in the world when everyone hates the smell of garbage?

156
01:27:15,920 --> 01:27:17,080
People who hate it the least.

157
01:27:17,080 --> 01:27:18,800
Yeah, people who hate it the least.

158
01:27:18,800 --> 01:27:23,160
So it's a nice intuitive result.

159
01:27:23,160 --> 01:27:25,600
Now, in an economist model, the economy overall,

160
01:27:25,600 --> 01:27:26,920
it's very common to say, well, let's

161
01:27:26,920 --> 01:27:28,480
assume that consumers are risk averse

162
01:27:28,480 --> 01:27:30,140
but firms are risk neutral.

163
01:27:30,140 --> 01:27:31,520
Although, when people actually go

164
01:27:31,520 --> 01:27:33,060
try to study what firms do, it seems

165
01:27:33,060 --> 01:27:35,560
that firms also act as if they are risk averse.

166
01:27:35,560 --> 01:27:39,280
Possibly because firms are run by humans.

167
01:27:39,280 --> 01:27:41,840
Interesting possibility.

168
01:27:41,840 --> 01:27:44,200
Although, you may have also heard there are cases where

169
01:27:44,200 --> 01:27:46,080
firms seem to be risk averse, risk aferring,

170
01:27:46,080 --> 01:27:48,360
or act as if they are risk aferring.

171
01:27:48,360 --> 01:27:52,840
Like, have you ever heard of zombie whatevers,

172
01:27:52,840 --> 01:27:55,240
like zombie SNLs?

173
01:27:55,240 --> 01:27:56,960
But again, normally, like I was saying,

174
01:27:56,960 --> 01:27:59,320
have you ever heard this expression, zombie SNLs?

175
01:27:59,320 --> 01:28:03,040
Basically, if you've got a firm where you're already

176
01:28:03,040 --> 01:28:04,760
like you're allowed to keep doing business

177
01:28:04,760 --> 01:28:07,800
even though you have negative net worth.

178
01:28:07,800 --> 01:28:11,720
So this is a situation where if you just accept the average,

179
01:28:11,720 --> 01:28:13,720
then you lose money for sure.

180
01:28:13,720 --> 01:28:16,040
Whereas, if you do a gamble, then either you

181
01:28:16,040 --> 01:28:19,160
will lose even more money, but there's limited liability,

182
01:28:19,160 --> 01:28:20,880
so no harm done.

183
01:28:20,880 --> 01:28:24,360
Or you will get lucky and then things will work out for you

184
01:28:24,360 --> 01:28:26,080
and then you will be back in the clear.

185
01:28:26,080 --> 01:28:28,840
So, anybody else?

186
01:28:28,840 --> 01:28:30,320
All right, so questions.

187
01:28:30,320 --> 01:28:33,080
Questions about insurance?

188
01:28:33,080 --> 01:28:34,400
Happy with insurance?

189
01:28:34,400 --> 01:28:36,680
Anyone want to start solving problems like this?

190
01:28:36,680 --> 01:28:38,760
If you're itching, as soon as class lights out,

191
01:28:38,760 --> 01:28:41,720
you will be solving insurance problems.

192
01:28:41,720 --> 01:28:44,760
That's the basic idea.

193
01:28:44,760 --> 01:28:51,160
Now, last topic for symmetric and perfect information.

194
01:28:51,160 --> 01:28:53,520
So there are a lot of textbooks that

195
01:28:53,520 --> 01:28:56,720
give a list of conditions under which markets fail.

196
01:28:56,720 --> 01:28:59,880
And a standard condition of market failure

197
01:28:59,880 --> 01:29:02,480
that you will see in many textbooks to this day

198
01:29:02,480 --> 01:29:04,520
is imperfect information.

199
01:29:04,520 --> 01:29:08,280
Information is imperfect and therefore markets fail.

200
01:29:08,280 --> 01:29:10,320
This turns out to be just a gross overstatement.

201
01:29:10,320 --> 01:29:12,600
It's just wrong.

202
01:29:12,600 --> 01:29:15,360
Because it's totally possible to combine market efficiency

203
01:29:15,360 --> 01:29:19,240
with symmetric and perfect information.

204
01:29:19,240 --> 01:29:21,520
And actually, as we're going to see,

205
01:29:21,520 --> 01:29:23,440
even when it's asymmetric, it might work out.

206
01:29:23,440 --> 01:29:25,680
But when it's symmetric and perfect information,

207
01:29:25,680 --> 01:29:28,480
and again, remember, that's when everyone is equally in the dark.

208
01:29:28,480 --> 01:29:32,360
There's no one who knows better than anybody else.

209
01:29:32,360 --> 01:29:34,280
And then it's very easy to show cases

210
01:29:34,280 --> 01:29:37,320
where, even though people have imperfect information,

211
01:29:37,320 --> 01:29:39,080
still everyone is acting optimally

212
01:29:39,080 --> 01:29:42,320
and there is no failure of any kind.

213
01:29:42,320 --> 01:29:44,720
So let's just go through some examples.

214
01:29:44,720 --> 01:29:46,360
So suppose I don't know how much I'm

215
01:29:46,360 --> 01:29:49,080
going to enjoy my consumption bundle.

216
01:29:49,080 --> 01:29:50,920
So I might have my utility function

217
01:29:50,920 --> 01:29:55,200
is going to be x to the a times y to the 1 minus a plus

218
01:29:55,200 --> 01:29:56,320
epsilon.

219
01:29:56,320 --> 01:29:59,720
Epsilon is normally distributed with mean 0.

220
01:29:59,720 --> 01:30:03,120
And what's my optimal decision?

221
01:30:03,120 --> 01:30:05,160
My optimal decision is exactly the same

222
01:30:05,160 --> 01:30:08,880
as if the epsilon were 0 all the time.

223
01:30:08,880 --> 01:30:11,480
So basically, what's happening here is you optimize.

224
01:30:11,480 --> 01:30:13,940
And then the universe just gives you either better or worse

225
01:30:13,940 --> 01:30:16,080
utility regardless of what you did.

226
01:30:16,080 --> 01:30:18,240
So in that case, there's no reason

227
01:30:18,240 --> 01:30:20,360
to think that there's any change.

228
01:30:20,360 --> 01:30:22,360
And there is no change in front of the outcome.

229
01:30:22,360 --> 01:30:24,320
So if the outcome is optimal before,

230
01:30:24,320 --> 01:30:27,560
then despite the imperfect information, remains optimal.

231
01:30:27,560 --> 01:30:30,920
Or it could be that suppose you don't know your relative taste

232
01:30:30,920 --> 01:30:32,280
for x and y.

233
01:30:32,280 --> 01:30:33,200
So this one's more fun.

234
01:30:33,200 --> 01:30:36,080
So suppose that you're maximizing, again,

235
01:30:36,080 --> 01:30:41,000
so you've got utility of x and y.

236
01:30:41,000 --> 01:30:43,120
And it's going to be given by x raised to the a,

237
01:30:43,120 --> 01:30:45,280
y raised to the 1 minus a.

238
01:30:45,280 --> 01:30:47,280
But then, what is a?

239
01:30:47,280 --> 01:30:49,960
Well, a could actually be unknown.

240
01:30:49,960 --> 01:30:54,840
So it could be that a equals 0.5 with a probability of 0.6.

241
01:30:54,840 --> 01:30:59,920
Or a could equal 0.9 with a probability of 0.4.

242
01:30:59,920 --> 01:31:01,760
And you're dealing with that.

243
01:31:01,760 --> 01:31:04,760
And again, then what's the optimal thing to do?

244
01:31:04,760 --> 01:31:08,000
The optimal thing to do is say, well, 60% chance

245
01:31:08,000 --> 01:31:10,920
we're in this world, 40% chance we're in that world.

246
01:31:10,920 --> 01:31:14,240
And I'll just go and do expect utility.

247
01:31:14,240 --> 01:31:19,080
And the fact that I am deeply confused about what I like

248
01:31:19,080 --> 01:31:21,240
doesn't lead to a suboptimal outcome.

249
01:31:21,240 --> 01:31:24,800
It leads to the optimal outcome.

250
01:31:24,800 --> 01:31:27,840
Now, this is only true if it's a scenario where, in this case,

251
01:31:27,840 --> 01:31:29,320
this needs to be symmetric.

252
01:31:29,320 --> 01:31:31,480
So now suppose that this is what I thought,

253
01:31:31,480 --> 01:31:33,560
but then the government knew, no, no, no.

254
01:31:33,560 --> 01:31:35,920
This is what your preference is for sure.

255
01:31:35,920 --> 01:31:37,040
I just know.

256
01:31:37,040 --> 01:31:37,720
You're an idiot.

257
01:31:37,720 --> 01:31:38,600
I know.

258
01:31:38,600 --> 01:31:40,040
In that case, then the government

259
01:31:40,040 --> 01:31:42,800
might be able to help you because it's not symmetric

260
01:31:42,800 --> 01:31:43,840
anymore.

261
01:31:43,840 --> 01:31:46,960
But if no one knows any more than you do,

262
01:31:46,960 --> 01:31:49,360
then there's no way of actually proving upon this outcome.

263
01:31:53,320 --> 01:31:55,200
And if you go to Robin Hanson, he'll even say,

264
01:31:55,200 --> 01:31:56,440
no, he wouldn't tell you, sir.

265
01:31:56,440 --> 01:31:59,160
There are theorems saying that as long as the information is

266
01:31:59,160 --> 01:32:02,320
truly symmetric, and again, that includes symmetric not just

267
01:32:02,320 --> 01:32:05,360
for market actors, but for all creatures.

268
01:32:05,360 --> 01:32:07,160
It's got to be symmetric for all creatures,

269
01:32:07,160 --> 01:32:08,640
including the government.

270
01:32:08,640 --> 01:32:11,880
But as long as it is symmetric, then there

271
01:32:11,880 --> 01:32:13,960
is no reason to think that the market outcome is

272
01:32:13,960 --> 01:32:16,680
any worse than normal.

273
01:32:16,680 --> 01:32:18,720
So it's just not true that a perfect information

274
01:32:18,720 --> 01:32:20,360
in general causes market failure.

275
01:32:22,960 --> 01:32:25,360
So the more general, so intuitively, here's

276
01:32:25,360 --> 01:32:28,160
the thing is, just because you're ignorant

277
01:32:28,160 --> 01:32:29,560
doesn't mean you're stupid.

278
01:32:29,560 --> 01:32:31,880
So if you're in this situation, you could either say, my god,

279
01:32:31,880 --> 01:32:33,760
I don't know what my own utility function is.

280
01:32:33,760 --> 01:32:34,440
What am I to do?

281
01:32:34,440 --> 01:32:35,200
What was me?

282
01:32:35,200 --> 01:32:37,080
I'll just act randomly.

283
01:32:37,080 --> 01:32:39,600
But that would not be what a sensible person would

284
01:32:39,600 --> 01:32:41,200
do based with this.

285
01:32:41,200 --> 01:32:43,360
The sensible thing is just to go and plug the numbers

286
01:32:43,360 --> 01:32:47,640
into expected utility theory and do that.

287
01:32:47,640 --> 01:32:49,280
That's a sensible thing.

288
01:32:49,280 --> 01:32:51,040
So anyway, so just because you're ignorant

289
01:32:51,040 --> 01:32:53,760
doesn't mean that you are stupid.

290
01:32:53,760 --> 01:32:56,520
So if you don't know exactly what's going on,

291
01:32:56,520 --> 01:33:00,640
you could just fall back on more general purpose strategies.

292
01:33:00,640 --> 01:33:02,720
Fall back on more general purpose strategies

293
01:33:02,720 --> 01:33:05,600
to take account of all the possible outcomes.

294
01:33:05,600 --> 01:33:07,120
Say, well, let's see.

295
01:33:07,120 --> 01:33:09,560
So this job seems more fun, but it's higher risk.

296
01:33:09,560 --> 01:33:12,720
So let me weigh the pros and cons.

297
01:33:12,720 --> 01:33:15,280
And again, if there's someone that actually

298
01:33:15,280 --> 01:33:18,360
knows better than you, then of course there is, well,

299
01:33:18,360 --> 01:33:20,600
how about just listen to them?

300
01:33:20,600 --> 01:33:23,480
But on the other hand, if nobody knows any better than you do,

301
01:33:23,480 --> 01:33:27,200
then whatever your best guess is is, as far as anyone knows,

302
01:33:27,200 --> 01:33:29,920
the best guess about what anyone should do.

303
01:33:29,920 --> 01:33:31,840
What you should do.

304
01:33:31,840 --> 01:33:35,280
And that is the solution.

305
01:33:35,280 --> 01:33:40,320
So we say, well, look, mountain climbing.

306
01:33:40,320 --> 01:33:41,880
Mountain climbing is a market failure

307
01:33:41,880 --> 01:33:43,420
because people don't know if they're

308
01:33:43,420 --> 01:33:45,280
going to make it to the top.

309
01:33:45,280 --> 01:33:51,160
Well, as long as they've got probabilities right,

310
01:33:51,160 --> 01:33:53,440
