Yeah, sure.

366
01:58:40,040 --> 01:58:43,040
Are there any other legal systems that don't?

367
01:58:43,040 --> 01:58:45,040
So, that I don't know, actually.

368
01:58:45,040 --> 01:58:52,040
So, the main other legal system is the civil law versus common law.

369
01:58:52,040 --> 01:58:56,040
It doesn't seem very civil law to be any better on this, but I don't know.

370
01:58:56,040 --> 01:58:57,040
All right.

371
01:58:57,040 --> 01:58:58,040
All right.

372
01:58:58,040 --> 01:59:00,040
So, that's moral hazard.

373
01:59:00,040 --> 01:59:03,040
And this moral hazard is the simplest form of asymmetric information,

374
01:59:03,040 --> 01:59:07,040
and it's one where the general idea was around long before economists were around.

375
01:59:07,040 --> 01:59:10,040
So, as long as it's been insurance, there have been people concerned about insurance fraud,

376
01:59:10,040 --> 01:59:13,040
which is the ultimate form of insurance of moral hazard.

377
01:59:13,040 --> 01:59:14,040
Right?

378
01:59:14,040 --> 01:59:16,040
So, the ultimate form of moral hazard is someone comes along and says,

379
01:59:16,040 --> 01:59:23,040
you know what, I'd like to buy a billion-dollar fire insurance policy on my shack.

380
01:59:23,040 --> 01:59:24,040
Right?

381
01:59:24,040 --> 01:59:25,040
And why?

382
01:59:25,040 --> 01:59:27,040
I just love that shack so much.

383
01:59:27,040 --> 01:59:31,040
I mean, you have no idea how miserable I would be without my shack.

384
01:59:31,040 --> 01:59:34,040
And every fire insurance company in the world will say,

385
01:59:34,040 --> 01:59:37,040
no, we're not going to insure your shack for a billion dollars.

386
01:59:37,040 --> 01:59:38,040
And why?

387
01:59:38,040 --> 01:59:41,040
Because if you just took people's word for it,

388
01:59:41,040 --> 01:59:45,040
then people would go and insure all kinds of junk for way more than it's worth

389
01:59:45,040 --> 01:59:50,040
and then would possibly destroy it on purpose for great financial gain.

390
01:59:50,040 --> 01:59:51,040
All right.

391
01:59:51,040 --> 01:59:54,040
Or if you know detective movies from the 30s and 40s,

392
01:59:54,040 --> 01:60:04,040
a standard movie is you get a life insurance policy on someone and then you murder them.


1
01:60:00,000 --> 01:60:03,700
So double indemnity, it's all about insurance policy.

2
01:60:03,700 --> 01:60:05,820
I guess so. Back in those days,

3
01:60:05,820 --> 01:60:09,100
insurance policies were considered very entertaining.

4
01:60:09,100 --> 01:60:14,180
And you can craft a very entertaining story around insurance policies.

5
01:60:14,180 --> 01:60:15,980
All right. So, anyway, so moral hazard,

6
01:60:15,980 --> 01:60:17,860
long known to the insurance industry.

7
01:60:17,860 --> 01:60:20,500
It's really as old as the insurance industry itself.

8
01:60:20,500 --> 01:60:25,780
Now, the next problem is one where insurance companies do things about it,

9
01:60:25,780 --> 01:60:28,580
and yet a lot of it seems like it's rule of thumb,

10
01:60:28,580 --> 01:60:30,220
and they don't really understand why they're doing it,

11
01:60:30,220 --> 01:60:32,100
and so economists actually have more value,

12
01:60:32,100 --> 01:60:33,660
or are adding more value.

13
01:60:33,660 --> 01:60:36,740
Maybe not in terms of the actual business behavior,

14
01:60:36,740 --> 01:60:39,460
but in terms of people actually understanding what's going on.

15
01:60:39,460 --> 01:60:42,540
It's a lot more, it's a lot subtler than moral hazard,

16
01:60:42,540 --> 01:60:45,980
which is pretty obvious once you think about it.

17
01:60:48,300 --> 01:60:55,700
So, this is another standard form of asymmetric information.

18
01:60:55,700 --> 01:60:57,780
All right. And the basic idea.

19
01:60:57,780 --> 01:60:59,740
So, you know your own characteristics.

20
01:60:59,740 --> 01:61:01,940
You know what you are like, right?

21
01:61:01,940 --> 01:61:03,620
But other people don't.

22
01:61:03,620 --> 01:61:08,060
So, a natural thing for other people to do is treat you as if you were

23
01:61:08,060 --> 01:61:13,220
the average member of a group that you superficially belong to.

24
01:61:13,860 --> 01:61:19,540
And which group you superficially belong to can change very easily.

25
01:61:19,540 --> 01:61:23,180
So, if I just go by myself to a playground,

26
01:61:23,180 --> 01:61:27,860
I am superficially similar to a child abductor.

27
01:61:27,860 --> 01:61:33,060
If I just wander onto a children's playground and just hang out there reading my book,

28
01:61:33,060 --> 01:61:34,860
people treat me in one way.

29
01:61:34,860 --> 01:61:39,580
I've never done this, but I assume it would give me bad looks.

30
01:61:39,580 --> 01:61:42,220
If I go there with a child on the other hand,

31
01:61:42,220 --> 01:61:43,660
they don't even know it's mine.

32
01:61:43,660 --> 01:61:45,380
But if I go there with a child,

33
01:61:45,380 --> 01:61:47,460
it's like there's a good father.

34
01:61:47,460 --> 01:61:49,180
Right now, in both cases,

35
01:61:49,180 --> 01:61:51,100
they really know next nothing about me,

36
01:61:51,100 --> 01:61:52,820
other than these very superficial things.

37
01:61:52,820 --> 01:61:57,300
One is, there is a man without a child in a playground.

38
01:61:57,300 --> 01:62:01,140
Or the other one is, there's a man with a child in a playground.

39
01:62:01,140 --> 01:62:06,500
Once I took my kids to do a test at a high school and school security came to me,

40
01:62:06,500 --> 01:62:10,780
and he said, a student came and said there was a man sitting here.

41
01:62:10,780 --> 01:62:17,580
There is. I didn't want to get in trouble,

42
01:62:17,580 --> 01:62:21,420
so I just said, guilty as charged, here I am.

43
01:62:21,420 --> 01:62:24,100
A man is sitting on a bench in a school.

44
01:62:24,100 --> 01:62:27,500
But instead, I'm waiting for my children.

45
01:62:27,500 --> 01:62:30,700
Okay. All right.

46
01:62:30,700 --> 01:62:33,860
So anyway, so you know what you are.

47
01:62:33,860 --> 01:62:35,980
You know whether you're planning on abducting anyone,

48
01:62:35,980 --> 01:62:40,740
but other people just take a quick look at you and treat you based upon

49
01:62:40,740 --> 01:62:45,260
the average characteristics of people that superficially resemble you.

50
01:62:45,260 --> 01:62:48,460
Now, here is the bad news.

51
01:62:48,460 --> 01:62:51,140
Suppose you're above average for that group.

52
01:62:51,140 --> 01:62:56,460
Then, you will be treated badly relative to the truth.

53
01:62:56,460 --> 01:62:58,180
So, and if there is a market,

54
01:62:58,180 --> 01:63:00,380
then you may be treated so badly,

55
01:63:00,380 --> 01:63:02,940
you decide you don't even feel like participating in the market.

56
01:63:02,940 --> 01:63:05,980
And if enough above average people think this,

57
01:63:05,980 --> 01:63:07,900
then the whole market can unravel.

58
01:63:07,900 --> 01:63:10,900
Because if the most above average people drop out,

59
01:63:10,900 --> 01:63:12,420
this lowers the average.

60
01:63:12,420 --> 01:63:16,900
And then the best of the people remaining are treated very badly,

61
01:63:16,900 --> 01:63:19,620
and they drop out, and then they drop out and so on.

62
01:63:19,620 --> 01:63:23,620
And imagine if I were to say that I'm going to fail the bottom half of this class.

63
01:63:23,620 --> 01:63:25,500
So, who would want to drop out?

64
01:63:25,500 --> 01:63:26,700
So, let's say this.

65
01:63:26,700 --> 01:63:29,300
Anyone who thinks they're going to be the bottom half wants to drop out.

66
01:63:29,300 --> 01:63:31,540
But this doesn't really solve the problem, does it?

67
01:63:31,540 --> 01:63:33,820
Because once they leave, then the class,

68
01:63:33,820 --> 01:63:34,940
then I said, well,

69
01:63:34,940 --> 01:63:37,580
the rule now I'm going to fail the bottom of whoever's left.

70
01:63:37,580 --> 01:63:39,580
And then more people may want to drop out,

71
01:63:39,580 --> 01:63:41,940
and then finally maybe left with one person.

72
01:63:41,940 --> 01:63:43,420
And then I don't know what the rule means.

73
01:63:43,420 --> 01:63:46,060
That's like 50-50 chance that they fail you or not.

74
01:63:46,060 --> 01:63:47,420
Whether we're down to one.

75
01:63:47,420 --> 01:63:54,140
But that would be unraveling where if the people that are in the bottom half,

76
01:63:54,140 --> 01:63:56,260
they'll just keep dropping out.

77
01:63:56,260 --> 01:63:59,660
And this makes the bottom half where we'll

78
01:63:59,660 --> 01:64:04,100
move the average and then finally go down with basically nobody.

79
01:64:04,100 --> 01:64:09,180
All right. So, that is the general problem of-

80
01:64:10,820 --> 01:64:13,740
over there. All right.

81
01:64:13,740 --> 01:64:18,220
So, there is a standard example that we've used many times.

82
01:64:18,220 --> 01:64:23,060
This is an example that's nice because it's so simple and it's so counterintuitive.

83
01:64:23,060 --> 01:64:26,220
And yet it's totally undeniable once you work through it.

84
01:64:26,220 --> 01:64:29,740
So, let's go through this class.

85
01:64:31,580 --> 01:64:35,300
Okay. So, suppose you've got a bunch of companies,

86
01:64:35,300 --> 01:64:39,740
and they are uniformly distributed on the interval 0 to 100.

87
01:64:39,740 --> 01:64:43,460
This is the value to the current owners of the companies.

88
01:64:43,460 --> 01:64:46,340
However, if you sell them,

89
01:64:46,340 --> 01:64:50,260
the buyers are able to run the companies more efficiently if you are.

90
01:64:50,260 --> 01:64:53,820
So, every company is worth 50 percent more in the hands of

91
01:64:53,820 --> 01:64:57,340
the buyer than it is in the hands of the current owner.

92
01:64:57,340 --> 01:64:59,700
Okay. All right.

93
01:64:59,700 --> 01:65:01,980
Now, what is going to happen?

94
01:65:01,980 --> 01:65:05,260
Okay. Well, let's see.

95
01:65:05,260 --> 01:65:08,180
Something that people often want to do is say,

96
01:65:08,180 --> 01:65:09,980
well, fine, I'll just bid, I'll bid 50.

97
01:65:09,980 --> 01:65:12,180
I'll bid the average value.

98
01:65:12,180 --> 01:65:14,260
So, I'll bid 50.

99
01:65:14,260 --> 01:65:16,620
And you intuitively think, well,

100
01:65:16,620 --> 01:65:19,860
this is going to work out really well for me because I will bid 50,

101
01:65:19,860 --> 01:65:22,420
and that's the average value of the company to the buyer,

102
01:65:22,420 --> 01:65:23,940
so they'll be happy to sell to me,

103
01:65:23,940 --> 01:65:25,660
but it's going to be worth 50 percent more to me,

104
01:65:25,660 --> 01:65:28,780
so I expect to make a profit of 25 on the company.

105
01:65:28,780 --> 01:65:31,020
That reasoning is totally wrong.

106
01:65:31,020 --> 01:65:33,620
And if we've got asymmetric information,

107
01:65:33,620 --> 01:65:37,260
it's because if the owners of the company know their true value,

108
01:65:37,260 --> 01:65:40,860
but all that you know is the average value of the company sold,

109
01:65:40,860 --> 01:65:43,060
something totally different will happen.

110
01:65:43,060 --> 01:65:44,900
Namely, if you bid 50,

111
01:65:44,900 --> 01:65:48,100
which companies will not be sold to you?

112
01:65:48,700 --> 01:65:52,780
All the companies worth more than 50 will not be sold to you.

113
01:65:52,780 --> 01:65:55,700
Those companies will be retained by the original owner,

114
01:65:55,700 --> 01:65:58,580
who know that they are worth more than 50.

115
01:65:58,580 --> 01:66:02,820
Therefore, the average value of the company sold to the owners,

116
01:66:02,820 --> 01:66:04,620
out of all the companies sold,

117
01:66:04,620 --> 01:66:07,460
the average value of the owners will be only 25.

118
01:66:07,460 --> 01:66:10,700
It's going to be worth, so the average company that sold will be worth 25.

119
01:66:10,700 --> 01:66:14,260
It's worth 50 percent more to you, so that's 37.5.

120
01:66:14,260 --> 01:66:17,900
This means that you are paying 50 to get a company,

121
01:66:17,900 --> 01:66:19,740
there's an average worth 37.5 to you,

122
01:66:19,740 --> 01:66:21,740
so you are losing money.

123
01:66:22,060 --> 01:66:25,100
All right. Now, what natural reaction is to say,

124
01:66:25,100 --> 01:66:27,460
fine, I'll just go and bid 100.

125
01:66:27,460 --> 01:66:30,380
We bid 100, then there's no longer any adverse selection.

126
01:66:30,380 --> 01:66:32,020
All the companies get sold.

127
01:66:32,020 --> 01:66:34,660
But in that case, you bid 100,

128
01:66:34,660 --> 01:66:36,620
the average value is 50.

129
01:66:36,620 --> 01:66:38,820
It's worth 50 percent more to you, so 75.

130
01:66:38,820 --> 01:66:42,500
So, you have actually lost even more money by increasing your bid.

131
01:66:42,500 --> 01:66:47,460
So, increasing your bid increases your loss even further.

132
01:66:47,460 --> 01:66:50,620
Then, the natural thing to do is to try bidding less.

133
01:66:50,620 --> 01:66:53,300
So, I say, fine, I'll just bid 25.

134
01:66:53,300 --> 01:66:56,660
But then, the average value will be 12.5,

135
01:66:56,660 --> 01:67:00,460
and the average value to the recipient will be 18.75.

136
01:67:00,460 --> 01:67:02,300
So, again, you've lost money.

137
01:67:02,300 --> 01:67:05,700
Sorry, fine, I'll go and bid two.

138
01:67:05,700 --> 01:67:08,140
If you bid two, the average value of the company sold is one,

139
01:67:08,140 --> 01:67:09,980
it's worth 1.5 to you, you've lost money.

140
01:67:09,980 --> 01:67:11,500
Although, every time you cut your bid,

141
01:67:11,500 --> 01:67:12,940
you are losing less money.

142
01:67:12,940 --> 01:67:14,300
That's a good sign, right?

143
01:67:14,300 --> 01:67:18,220
Because losing less is reducing your loss is better.

144
01:67:18,220 --> 01:67:21,020
Then, finally, you may say, fine, how about I bid zero?

145
01:67:21,020 --> 01:67:23,900
You bid zero, no companies are sold to you,

146
01:67:23,900 --> 01:67:26,220
you pay nothing, they're worth nothing,

147
01:67:26,220 --> 01:67:27,700
and you have now reached

148
01:67:27,700 --> 01:67:30,300
the profit maximizing solution of this problem.

149
01:67:30,300 --> 01:67:33,740
Buying no companies and losing no money is

150
01:67:33,740 --> 01:67:37,700
the best possible outcome given the setup of the problem.

151
01:67:37,700 --> 01:67:40,620
That clear to everyone?

152
01:67:40,620 --> 01:67:44,660
So, this is an extreme case of adverse selection,

153
01:67:44,660 --> 01:67:46,580
and this is one where there is full unraveling.

154
01:67:46,580 --> 01:67:48,860
So, people fully understand what's going on.

155
01:67:48,860 --> 01:67:50,180
No companies will be sold,

156
01:67:50,180 --> 01:67:52,100
even though every company is worth more to

157
01:67:52,100 --> 01:67:54,460
someone else than it is to the person that holds it.

158
01:67:54,460 --> 01:67:56,820
That's what's so odd.

159
01:67:56,820 --> 01:67:59,300
Okay. So, that's the equilibrium.

160
01:67:59,300 --> 01:68:01,740
Now, if there were symmetric information,

161
01:68:01,740 --> 01:68:03,860
there'd be a totally different result.

162
01:68:03,860 --> 01:68:06,780
If there were symmetric equilibrium,

163
01:68:06,780 --> 01:68:08,420
then there would be a different price for

164
01:68:08,420 --> 01:68:11,100
every company and basically you just pay everybody.

165
01:68:11,100 --> 01:68:13,740
You can offer everybody the value of their company,

166
01:68:13,740 --> 01:68:14,940
or you can offer everybody, say,

167
01:68:14,940 --> 01:68:16,820
10 percent more than the value of the company,

168
01:68:16,820 --> 01:68:19,940
and then all the companies will be sold.

169
01:68:19,940 --> 01:68:22,900
So, with symmetric information,

170
01:68:22,900 --> 01:68:24,900
this would be a perfectly efficient markets.

171
01:68:24,900 --> 01:68:26,820
Confirming that plan is telling you that

172
01:68:26,820 --> 01:68:29,100
symmetric and perfect information by itself,

173
01:68:29,100 --> 01:68:29,980
the ratchet goes over and over again.

174
01:68:29,980 --> 01:68:31,180
That was perfect information.

175
01:68:31,180 --> 01:68:35,140
Now, symmetric and perfect information would be,

176
01:68:35,140 --> 01:68:38,780
if the sellers don't know the value of their company either.

177
01:68:38,780 --> 01:68:41,940
All right. So, suppose that neither buyer

178
01:68:41,940 --> 01:68:44,580
nor seller knows whether the company is any good.

179
01:68:44,580 --> 01:68:48,340
In that case, the seller could go and offer 50,

180
01:68:48,340 --> 01:68:52,900
everyone sells, and therefore,

181
01:68:52,900 --> 01:68:56,940
the average buyer winds up getting a company worth 75.

182
01:68:56,940 --> 01:69:00,380
Or you could say, so offer 51,

183
01:69:00,380 --> 01:69:02,260
say, just to give a slight amount of

184
01:69:02,260 --> 01:69:04,900
a slight share of the profit to the original owners.

185
01:69:04,900 --> 01:69:07,820
But again, if the owners don't know if their company is any good,

186
01:69:07,820 --> 01:69:12,420
and if the buyers don't know if the company is any good,

187
01:69:12,420 --> 01:69:15,540
then you actually get a better outcome.

188
01:69:16,100 --> 01:69:20,220
Which, if you think about it, is counterintuitive.

189
01:69:20,220 --> 01:69:22,620
Because this is saying that,

190
01:69:22,620 --> 01:69:24,980
we start out the world where some people

191
01:69:24,980 --> 01:69:27,940
know they have perfect information and others don't,

192
01:69:27,940 --> 01:69:30,300
we actually get a terrible outcome.

193
01:69:30,300 --> 01:69:34,740
Whereas, if we go and mind wipe the sellers,

194
01:69:34,740 --> 01:69:37,940
we actually move to not only a better outcome,

195
01:69:37,940 --> 01:69:42,380
we can move to a fully efficient outcome by just deleting the information.

196
01:69:42,380 --> 01:69:45,740
So, not only does this mean that

197
01:69:45,740 --> 01:69:49,220
you can get a totally efficient result with the perfect information,

198
01:69:49,220 --> 01:69:54,300
you can actually solve a market failure by reducing information.

199
01:69:54,300 --> 01:69:57,100
It's completely true.

200
01:69:57,100 --> 01:69:59,500
Again, what's the solution?

201
01:69:59,500 --> 01:70:01,300
Now, again, of course, if there's

202
01:70:01,300 --> 01:70:02,260
perfect information for everyone,

203
01:70:02,260 --> 01:70:04,620
then there's no problem in this market. That's fine.

204
01:70:04,620 --> 01:70:07,340
But, and if there is,

205
01:70:07,340 --> 01:70:09,780
even if there's some metric imperfect, that's fine.

206
01:70:09,780 --> 01:70:12,180
The inefficiency comes from the asymmetry.

207
01:70:12,180 --> 01:70:14,420
If some people know and some don't,

208
01:70:14,420 --> 01:70:16,500
then this leads to strategic behavior,

209
01:70:16,500 --> 01:70:19,500
which leads to a socially inefficient outcome.

210
01:70:20,260 --> 01:70:22,820
Everyone clear on that?

211
01:70:22,820 --> 01:70:24,460
And again, understand the mind wipes.

212
01:70:24,460 --> 01:70:27,780
This means that we can actually make some people know less,

213
01:70:27,780 --> 01:70:29,300
the world would work better.

214
01:70:29,300 --> 01:70:31,780
And in fact, you can make the world work perfectly.

215
01:70:31,780 --> 01:70:37,100
If no one knew how good the countries were.

216
01:70:37,100 --> 01:70:41,900
Surprising. Questions about that?

217
01:70:41,900 --> 01:70:45,620
Is it too late to absorb how crazy that is?

218
01:70:45,620 --> 01:70:47,820
Amazing that is? Yeah.

219
01:70:47,820 --> 01:70:49,700
I mean, when you think about it more,

220
01:70:49,700 --> 01:70:51,860
it's like you come up with those lots of examples.

221
01:70:51,860 --> 01:70:55,740
Like, in general, can error make the world work better?

222
01:70:55,740 --> 01:70:59,740
There's all kinds of errors which would be great if they were widely shared.

223
01:70:59,740 --> 01:71:02,060
Like, suppose there was a view that if you murder anyone,

224
01:71:02,060 --> 01:71:03,940
you will explode instantly.

225
01:71:03,940 --> 01:71:06,020
This would basically abolish murder.

226
